WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.335
-Our next guest
is the editor-in-chief

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:05.572
of "T: The New York Times
Style Magazine"

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:06.940
and the award-winning author

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.708
of the celebrated novel
"A Little Life."

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:11.711
Her new novel, "To Paradise,"
was published today.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.980
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Hanya Yanagihara.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.150
Hello, Hanya, how are you?

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.686
-Hi, Seth. How are you?
Are you still sick?

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:22.822
-I'm still --
I think I'm at the end of it.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.990
I never really had any symptoms.
-Right.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.659
-So ultimately I'm doing great,

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:29.562
and it's just nice to be
in a living room.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.131
-Yeah, it is.
I mean, I was -- You know,

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:33.433
I was saying that
the nice thing about this

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.802
is I don't have to worry
about physical coordination.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:39.338
When I came on your show seven
years ago, the entire time,

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:41.707
all I could think about was,
"Am I gonna fall?"

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:43.509
And I didn't,
so it was a major success,

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:45.711
and now I don't have to
think about that at all.

00:00:45.711 --> 00:00:46.846
-That's -- When you left,

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:49.849
all we talked about
was how little you fell.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.250
-[ Laughs ]
Right, right. Right.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.686
It was a major accomplishment
of the night.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.688
-Now, it's very nice
to have you as a guest

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:58.791
for the second time on our show,
especially since you have said

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:00.460
the first time
you were asked on this show

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.195
you thought it was
a practical joke.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:05.598
-Yeah, I mean, it just seemed
like such a horrible idea

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:08.735
to have authors
on a late-night show.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.537
And I have to say
I'm so grateful to you

00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:13.506
and, you know,
to your producer, Sarah,

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:16.075
because you guys
gave my book a life.

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:18.277
And I know I don't speak
just for myself,

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:20.546
but for so many other
fiction writers

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:22.148
that you have on the show,
as well.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.584
I mean, this is --
You know, it's hard

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:28.287
to get people to read novels,
and I'm so grateful to you.

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:30.823
It's a real honor to be back.

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:33.226
-Well, I think that
with or without our show,

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.529
people were going to read "A
Little Life," your last novel.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:39.932
Over almost I believe
2 million copies sold worldwide.

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:43.469
And people, you know,
based on your wonderful work,

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:44.904
have a very strong reaction,

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.706
a very strong connection
to the book,

00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:49.809
and some people have reached
out to you with notes in a way

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.478
that maybe is
a little surprising

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:55.948
insofar as how they wanted you
to see their connection to it.

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:57.650
-I mean, people have been
very, very nice,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.921
and it's been such an honor
to get to interact with readers.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:03.455
It doesn't happen that often,

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:07.293
and so when it does happen,
it really makes my day.

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.795
But a few times,
I've gotten these posts,

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:12.332
you know,
on DM over Instagram,

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.200
and it'll just be
a picture of a guy --

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.568
it's always a guy --

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:19.172
holding a copy of
"A Little Life" over his junk.

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:22.207
And he kind of looks sad.
I don't know what --

00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:24.777
I don't know who the audience is
or what the message is.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.246
It never has a message.
It's just the picture.

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:29.015
And I always write back
"Thank you," you know,

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.784
because it takes a lot of time
to set up an image like that.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.452
You know, you've got to adjust
the ring lights

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:35.922
and you've got to get the angles
or you've got to get someone

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.791
to do it for you, so a lot of,
you know, art direction

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:41.327
has gone into the shot,
and I do appreciate it.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.595
I mean, I often think,
"Is this actually for me

00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:46.666
or was this for, you know,
your dating profile

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:48.768
or your writer profile
or whatever?"

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.538
But it gets sent to me, and
I am very grateful for it, too.

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:54.006
-That's very -- I think any time
you can connect with your fans

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:55.741
on a one-to-one level...
-That's right.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:58.210
That's right.
Yeah, yeah. A level.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.746
We're not sure
which level, though.

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:01.147
-Exactly.

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:04.250
This, your new book,
is very different,

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.487
and I'm wondering
if you approached it thinking,

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:09.322
"I have to do
something different,"

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:13.025
or if it was just the book
you wanted to write next?

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:15.328
-Yeah, it really was just
the book I wanted to write next.

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:18.630
I think you spend so long
working on a novel --

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.533
you know, I spent three years
start to finish

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.468
in the actual writing
of this book --

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:25.405
that it has to feel something --
like something that's urgent

00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:27.974
and that only you can say,
and it can't be a repeat.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:29.541
You know, it has to be something

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:31.977
that you really want
to spend time with.

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:33.812
And I think that the reader
also senses

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:35.582
when you're trying
to pander to her

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.983
and when you're trying to make
the same kind of experience,

00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:39.619
and it almost never works.

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:41.587
I mean, sequels
are very, very hard to do,

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.324
and there has to be
a real reason for doing them,

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:46.125
and there wasn't, really,
for "A Little Life."

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:49.128
So it is a very different book,
although I think emotionally

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:53.799
a lot of the themes and motifs
are the same.

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:55.167
-You talk about urgency

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:58.304
and writing about a book
that fits the time.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.706
There is a pandemic element
of this book,

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:05.578
but you began it
before the pandemic.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:06.746
In a way, you sort of --

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.781
I mean, I don't want to say
you predicted it,

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.883
but it must have been strange
to work on the book

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.385
through a pandemic
having already

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:14.386
started a book about a pandemic.

00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:17.223
-Yeah, I mean, listen,
if it had been 400 years ago,

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.592
I probably would have been
burned at a stake somewhere,

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:22.695
but I don't have any
particular predictive powers.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.563
And the funny thing was,
you know,

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:28.367
by the time we all got sent home
on March 13th of 2020,

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.936
I was so deep into the pandemic
part of this book,

00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:32.872
which is the last part
of the book,

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.806
that it felt very comforting,

00:04:34.806 --> 00:04:37.443
because outside
in the real world

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:38.944
I was a citizen with --

00:04:38.944 --> 00:04:41.146
who was just as confused
and scared

00:04:41.146 --> 00:04:43.049
and misinformed
as everyone else.

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:44.817
But inside the world
of the book,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.686
I got to do
exactly what I wanted.

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.687
I had an ultimate sense
of control,

00:04:48.687 --> 00:04:53.125
and it was reassuring in a way
to have a project

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:55.628
that felt
that I had created a world

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:58.197
and that world
was within my grasp,

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:02.601
when, of course, that wasn't
happening in real life at all.

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:05.137
-You know, I think
that naturally people

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:07.773
are gonna draw the parallel
between the pandemic in the book

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.575
and the pandemic
we're living through,

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.911
but a theme
that I feel resonates

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:13.813
even more for the time
we're living through

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:17.783
is the idea of who gets
to tell our history

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:20.920
and who gets to frame the times
we've lived through.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.558
I mean, we see right now how,
a year ago,

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:28.393
there's this deep fight
about what actually happened,

00:05:28.393 --> 00:05:32.064
and you just realize
that people are so thirsty

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.633
to tell the story
they want to tell

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:36.969
and make that
the accepted fact.

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:38.337
-Yes.
-Is that something,

00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:41.473
as you were writing,
that you felt was going on?

00:05:41.473 --> 00:05:43.142
-I mean,
more than the pandemic,

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.277
the thing that really
surprised me was that,

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:48.214
as you said, the conversations
that the book has

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:51.450
are the ones that we are now
having in real life

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.186
on a much broader scale
than perhaps we ever have.

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:55.788
You know, so, the book is set

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:58.157
in three different versions
of America --

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:01.727
one in 1893, one in 1993,
and one in 2093.

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:03.329
But running through
all of those sections

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:05.831
is the question of,
what is America,

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:08.867
who tells our history,
who's left out of the history.

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:12.438
You know, is America a country
that is great but flawed

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.806
and can be fixed
within the systems that exist?

00:06:14.806 --> 00:06:17.710
Or is America a country
that is irreparably damaged

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.145
and can only be --

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:21.947
You know, it can only be
burnt down and started over?

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:24.316
Or is America
the best it's ever been?

00:06:24.316 --> 00:06:27.786
You know, these competing ideas
of what this country is

00:06:27.786 --> 00:06:29.255
and how we understand
its history

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:30.756
and who has been
written out of it

00:06:30.756 --> 00:06:32.891
and who has been written into it
are questions

00:06:32.891 --> 00:06:35.494
and conversations
we're having in schools,

00:06:35.494 --> 00:06:38.030
on a policy level,
in journalism,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.699
and amongst friends and family.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:43.102
And that was not something
that we were having

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:46.105
when I began writing this
in, you know, 2017.

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:47.773
We were having the beginnings
of them,

00:06:47.773 --> 00:06:49.474
but we weren't having them
on the scale

00:06:49.474 --> 00:06:52.778
we're having them on now.

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:55.314
-I want to ask you
one last question, Hanya.

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:57.950
Because I read this
in an article --

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.186
that in college
you were invited to an orgy.

00:07:01.186 --> 00:07:03.722
First of all, congratulations.
That never happened to me.

00:07:03.722 --> 00:07:06.758
You did not go,
and the reason you didn't go

00:07:06.758 --> 00:07:10.529
I find both very relatable
and deeply funny.

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:12.898
Will you please tell everyone
why you turned down

00:07:12.898 --> 00:07:15.367
an invitation to a college orgy?
-Well, yes.

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:17.236
I mean, the first thing
I have to say, though,

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:19.338
is, you know,
my boss just wrote me a note

00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:21.306
about this profile
in "The New Yorker"

00:07:21.306 --> 00:07:22.708
and said, "Good profile."

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:24.543
But you don't want to think
about your boss,

00:07:24.543 --> 00:07:26.745
especially when your boss
is the executive editor

00:07:26.745 --> 00:07:29.248
of "The New York Times,"
reading about, you know,

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:31.650
your possibly going
to an orgy in college.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:34.786
But I had these very messy
next-door neighbors,

00:07:34.786 --> 00:07:38.791
and they were great people and
they hosted an orgy every year.

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:40.693
But they could not
clean up anything,

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:43.161
so they would let it go for
months and months and months,

00:07:43.161 --> 00:07:44.697
and then I would have to
help them clean up.

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:47.332
And I knew if I went, I'd have
to be involved in the cleanup,

00:07:47.332 --> 00:07:49.067
so I didn't go.

00:07:49.067 --> 00:07:51.137
And, you know, I still
had to do the cleanup anyway,

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:52.938
but that's very relatable,
right?

00:07:52.938 --> 00:07:57.076
-It's very forward-thinking
to get invited to an orgy

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:58.611
and just say, "You know what?

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:00.312
This orgy's gonna end
at some point,

00:08:00.312 --> 00:08:02.548
and there's gonna be a mess."
-That's right.

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:05.718
It is a real time-cost,
you know, assessment

00:08:05.718 --> 00:08:08.220
that you have to make
in the moment.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.288
-Well, I think
you made the right call.

00:08:10.288 --> 00:08:12.592
And congratulations
on another wonderful book.

00:08:12.592 --> 00:08:13.892
Thanks so much for being here,
Hanya.

00:08:13.892 --> 00:08:16.829
-Thank you so much.
Thank you for the honor, Seth.

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:19.398
-"To Paradise" is available
now wherever you buy your books,

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:21.200
but please remember to support
local and

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.536
independent bookstores.
We'll be right back.

